#: ../../user_2fa.rst:3
msgid "Using two-factor authentication"
msgstr ""

#: ../../user_2fa.rst:5
msgid "Two-factor authentication (2FA) is a way to protect your Nextcloud account against unauthorized access. It works by requiring two different 'proofs' of your identity. For example, *something you know* (like a password) and *something you have* like a physical key. Typically, the first factor is a password like you already have and the second can be a text message you receive or a code you generate on your phone or another device (*something you have*). Nextcloud supports a variety of 2nd factors and more can be added."
msgstr ""

#: ../../user_2fa.rst:14
msgid "Once a two-factor authentication app has been enabled by your administrator, you can enable and configure it in :doc:`userpreferences`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../user_2fa.rst:18
msgid "Configuring two-factor authentication"
msgstr ""

#: ../../user_2fa.rst:20
msgid "In your personal settings, look up the **Second-factor Auth** setting. In this example this is TOTP, a Google Authenticator compatible time-based code:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../user_2fa.rst:23
msgid "TOTP configuration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../user_2fa.rst:26
msgid "You will see your secret and a QR code which can be scanned by the TOTP app on your phone (or another device). Depending on the app or tool, type in the code or scan the QR and your device will show a login code which changes every 30 seconds."
msgstr ""

#: ../../user_2fa.rst:32
msgid "Recovery codes if you lose your second factor"
msgstr ""

#: ../../user_2fa.rst:34
msgid "You should always generate backup codes for 2FA. If your second factor device gets stolen or stops working, you can use one of these codes to unlock your account. It effectively functions as a backup second factor. To get the backup codes, go to your personal settings and look under **Second-factor Auth** settings. Choose *Generate backup codes*:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../user_2fa.rst:40
msgid "2FA backup code generator"
msgstr ""

#: ../../user_2fa.rst:43
msgid "You will then see a list of one-time-use backup codes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../user_2fa.rst:45
msgid "2FA backup codes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../user_2fa.rst:48
msgid "Store these codes somewhere safe where you can find them. Do not keep them with your second factor (such as your mobile phone), but store them separately so that losing one does not mean losing the other."
msgstr ""

#: ../../user_2fa.rst:53
msgid "Logging in with two-factor authentication"
msgstr ""

#: ../../user_2fa.rst:55
msgid "After you log out and log in again, you will see a prompt to enter the TOTP code in your browser. If you have enabled more than one second factor, you will see a selection screen where you can choose which method to use for this login. Select TOTP:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../user_2fa.rst:60
msgid "Choosing a two-factor authentication method."
msgstr ""

#: ../../user_2fa.rst:63
msgid "Now, just enter your code:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../user_2fa.rst:65
msgid "Entering TOTP code at login."
msgstr ""

#: ../../user_2fa.rst:68
msgid "If the code was correct you will be redirected to your Nextcloud account."
msgstr ""

#: ../../user_2fa.rst:71
msgid "Since the code is time-based, it is important that your server's and your smartphone's clock are almost in sync. A time drift of a few seconds won't be a problem."
msgstr ""

